.\" ctermid.3 - get the pathname of the controlling terminal
.TH CTERMID 3 "2026-09-13" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
ctermid \- get the pathname of the controlling terminal
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "char *ctermid(char *" s ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR ctermid ()
function returns a pathname that, when opened, refers to the controlling
terminal of the calling process.
On Substrate that pathname is always
.IR /dev/tty ,
which the device filesystem resolves to the opener's controlling terminal.
.PP
If
.I s
is not NULL, it must point to an array of at least
.B L_ctermid
bytes, and the pathname is stored there.
If
.I s
is NULL, the pathname is stored in a static buffer that later calls
overwrite.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR ctermid ()
function returns
.I s
if it is not NULL, and otherwise a pointer to its static buffer.
It always succeeds and does not set
.IR errno .
.SH NOTES
A returned name does not mean the process has a controlling terminal.
Opening it fails in that case, and that is how a caller finds out.
.PP
With a NULL argument
.BR ctermid ()
is not thread-safe, because every call shares the one static buffer.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following program opens the controlling terminal, so it can prompt even
when standard input and output are redirected.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
main(void)
{
    char name[L_ctermid];
    int fd = open(ctermid(name), O_RDWR);

    if (fd == \-1) {
        perror(name);
        return 1;
    }
    write(fd, "Continue? ", strlen("Continue? "));
    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (2),
.BR ttyname (3)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2008.
